questionvariables.text:
/* Pool rows: [id, argument, method, likely error, other wrong token, lead for the likely error, explanation]. */
pool: [
 ["D1", "Multiplying out, \\((x - 1)(x + 1) = x^2 + x - x - 1 = x^2 - 1\\), so the two sides are equal for every real number \\(x\\).", dir, exh, ind,
  "Nothing here is split into cases or checked value by value.",
  "It starts from rules of algebra that are always true and reaches the conclusion in a chain of steps: no cases, nothing assumed false, no example. A direct proof."],
 ["D2", "Let \\(n\\) be an even number, so \\(n = 2k\\) for some integer \\(k\\). Then \\(n^2 = 4k^2 = 2(2k^2)\\), so \\(n^2\\) is even.", dir, ind, exh,
  "There is no first case here, and no step from \\(k\\) to \\(k + 1\\).",
  "It starts from what is given, that \\(n\\) is even, and deduces the conclusion step by step for any even \\(n\\). A direct proof."],
 ["E1", "The statement is only about \\(n = 1, 2, 3\\) and \\(4\\), so we check it for each of these four values in turn.", exh, ind, cex,
  "Checking four values one at a time is not induction: there is no step from each case to the next.",
  "The statement is about four values only, and each is checked. Checking values is a proof when the list of cases is finite and complete: a proof by exhaustion."],
 ["E2", "Every integer \\(n\\) is even or odd. If \\(n = 2k\\), then \\(n^2 + n = 2k(2k + 1)\\), which is even. If \\(n = 2k + 1\\), then \\(n^2 + n = (2k + 1)(2k + 2) = 2(2k + 1)(k + 1)\\), which is even. So \\(n^2 + n\\) is always even.", exh, dir, con,
  "Look at the structure: the argument splits into two cases.",
  "It splits the integers into a finite number of cases, even and odd, and proves the statement in each: a proof by exhaustion."],
 ["I1", "A formula is checked for \\(n = 1\\). Then it is shown that whenever the formula holds for \\(n = k\\), it also holds for \\(n = k + 1\\). So it holds for every positive integer \\(n\\).", ind, exh, dir,
  "The cases are not checked one at a time: one step covers them all.",
  "A first case, then a step from each case to the next, which together reach every positive integer: a proof by induction."],
 ["I2", "\\(3^1 - 1 = 2\\) is even. If \\(3^k - 1\\) is even, then \\(3^{k+1} - 1 = 3(3^k - 1) + 2\\) is even too. So \\(3^n - 1\\) is even for every positive integer \\(n\\).", ind, dir, exh,
  "Look at the shape: a first case, then a step from \\(n = k\\) to \\(n = k + 1\\).",
  "It checks \\(n = 1\\), then shows that if the statement holds for \\(n = k\\) it holds for \\(n = k + 1\\): a proof by induction."],
 ["C1", "Suppose that there is a largest even number, \\(N\\). Then \\(N + 2\\) is even and larger than \\(N\\), which is impossible. So there is no largest even number.", con, cex, dir,
  "This is not an example where a claim fails: something is assumed and then shown to be impossible.",
  "It assumes the opposite of what is to be proved and shows that this leads to something impossible: a proof by contradiction."],
 ["C2", "Suppose that \\(\\sqrt{2} = \\dfrac{p}{q}\\), where \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are integers with no common factor. Then \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) must both be even, which is impossible. So \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) is irrational.", con, dir, exh,
  "Look at how the argument starts: it supposes the opposite of what it proves.",
  "It begins by supposing that \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) is rational, the opposite of the statement, and reaches something impossible: a proof by contradiction."],
 ["X1", "When \\(a = 4\\) and \\(b = 1\\), \\(a^3 + b^3 = 65\\) but \\((a + b)^3 = 125\\). So the claim that \\(a^3 + b^3 = (a + b)^3\\) for all real numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is false.", cex, exh, dir,
  "One value is checked, and it shows that the claim fails.",
  "One example where the claim fails shows that it is false: a disproof by counterexample. One value cannot prove a claim, but it can disprove one."],
 ["X2", "The claim that every prime number is odd is false, because \\(2\\) is a prime number and \\(2\\) is even.", cex, con, ind,
  "Nothing is assumed and then contradicted: one example shows that the claim fails.",
  "One case where the claim fails, the prime 2, is enough to show that it is false: a disproof by counterexample."]
];
rowsof(m) := sublist(pool, lambda([rw], is(rw[3] = m)));
/* Four of the five methods, one row for each, in a random order. */
meths: rand_selection([dir, exh, ind, con, cex], 4);
pick: [rand(rowsof(meths[1])), rand(rowsof(meths[2])), rand(rowsof(meths[3])), rand(rowsof(meths[4]))];
qrows: random_permutation(pick);
dd(c) := [[dir, is(c = dir), "direct proof"], [exh, is(c = exh), "proof by exhaustion"], [ind, is(c = ind), "proof by induction"], [con, is(c = con), "proof by contradiction"], [cex, is(c = cex), "disproof by counterexample"]];
arg1: qrows[1][2]; cor1: qrows[1][3]; diag1: qrows[1][4]; othr1: qrows[1][5]; lead1: qrows[1][6]; expl1: qrows[1][7];
arg2: qrows[2][2]; cor2: qrows[2][3]; diag2: qrows[2][4]; othr2: qrows[2][5]; lead2: qrows[2][6]; expl2: qrows[2][7];
arg3: qrows[3][2]; cor3: qrows[3][3]; diag3: qrows[3][4]; othr3: qrows[3][5]; lead3: qrows[3][6]; expl3: qrows[3][7];
arg4: qrows[4][2]; cor4: qrows[4][3]; diag4: qrows[4][4]; othr4: qrows[4][5]; lead4: qrows[4][6]; expl4: qrows[4][7];
ta1: dd(cor1); ta2: dd(cor2); ta3: dd(cor3); ta4: dd(cor4);
s_assert(is(length(unique([cor1, cor2, cor3, cor4])) = 4), true);
